WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.936 --> 00:00:12.523
Hi, everybody.

2
00:00:13.324 --> 00:00:16.927
I only have a few minutes to talk because Alex and Melissa are about to come over.

3
00:00:17.867 --> 00:00:19.309
We're working on a project for Mr.

4
00:00:19.329 --> 00:00:24.132
Fay's class, where we just learned about the Serengeti, a savanna plain in eastern Africa.

5
00:00:25.173 --> 00:00:28.316
Our assignment is to make a poster advertising a safari tour.

6
00:00:29.336 --> 00:00:33.480
We have to include interesting things about the Serengeti that will make people want to go on this

7
00:00:33.540 --> 00:00:39.925
tour. We learned tons of cool things in class, but here's my favorite: every year, for the past

8
00:00:40.025 --> 00:00:44.749
two million years or so, the Serengeti hosts one of the largest migrations on earth.

9
00:00:45.750 --> 00:00:51.035
Over a million wildebeests stampede over the grassy plains in search of water and food!

10
00:00:52.776 --> 00:00:57.000
Did you know that humans have lived in the Serengeti for over two million years too?

11
00:00:58.144 --> 00:01:03.708
Mr. Fay told us that the landscapes, animals, and climate of the Serengeti haven't really changed much in

12
00:01:03.768 --> 00:01:04.509
all these years.

13
00:01:05.690 --> 00:01:09.752
So if you visit today, you can easily imagine what it looked like a million years ago.

14
00:01:10.833 --> 00:01:16.257
The Serengeti covers nearly thirty thousand square kilometers and contains a variety of landscapes.

15
00:01:17.438 --> 00:01:22.221
It is most famous for its endless grassy plains, which seem to go on and on forever.

16
00:01:22.902 --> 00:01:25.644
But there are also rivers, forests, and volcanoes.

17
00:01:26.816 --> 00:01:31.440
Let me tell you about the wildebeests' migration, since that will be the main feature of our poster.

18
00:01:33.141 --> 00:01:38.766
The wildebeests live in the volcanic plains of the southern Serengeti from December to June, which is the

19
00:01:38.806 --> 00:01:39.486
rainy season.

20
00:01:40.547 --> 00:01:45.111
They need water to drink and food to eat, which are plentiful, as long as there's rain.

21
00:01:46.052 --> 00:01:51.416
But after June the southern Serengeti becomes desert-like as the hot sun dries up the soil.

22
00:01:52.437 --> 00:01:59.226
So the wildebeests—nearly two million of them—travel west, then north, then south in search of rain until they

23
00:01:59.266 --> 00:02:01.748
come back, full circle, a year later.

24
00:02:02.848 --> 00:02:04.109
So how do they know where to go?

25
00:02:04.970 --> 00:02:10.253
Well, scientists have figured out that wildebeests can detect rain from up to fifty kilometers away.

26
00:02:11.254 --> 00:02:13.355
But nobody knows exactly how they do this.

27
00:02:15.177 --> 00:02:19.840
Scientists do know that the wildebeests are a crucial part of the Serengeti's ecosystem.

28
00:02:20.760 --> 00:02:27.044
During their migration the animals act as natural lawn mowers and fertilizers by eating grass, digging up soil

29
00:02:27.084 --> 00:02:30.145
with their stampeding hooves, and then depositing their waste.

30
00:02:31.186 --> 00:02:34.447
Mr. Fay described their waste as a daily present to the Earth.

31
00:02:35.527 --> 00:02:39.149
And guess how much that daily present weighs?

32
00:02:39.229 --> 00:02:40.149
420 tons!

33
00:02:41.109 --> 00:02:42.570
That is a lot of dung!

34
00:02:43.690 --> 00:02:48.912
The wildebeests are awesome, but no tour of the Serengeti would be complete without the "Big Five."

35
00:02:49.984 --> 00:02:54.946
These are the five animals that are so dangerous, they've been known since ancient times as the beasts

36
00:02:54.986 --> 00:03:00.967
that hunt the hunters: lions, leopards, rhinos, buffalo, and elephants.

37
00:03:02.248 --> 00:03:06.469
The Big Five are huge animals that don't like humans interfering with their habitat.

38
00:03:08.190 --> 00:03:13.651
If you ever visit the Serengeti, be prepared to wake up to the frightening roar of leopards or

39
00:03:13.691 --> 00:03:16.232
the grunt of an angry buffalo outside your tent!

40
00:03:17.312 --> 00:03:22.697
And you should definitely know what to do if you see a 5,000-kilogram elephant flapping its ears at

41
00:03:22.737 --> 00:03:28.381
you. Run and climb the nearest tree because that means the elephant is about to charge!

42
00:03:29.582 --> 00:03:33.826
So you might be wondering how humans could possibly live in a place like the Serengeti.

43
00:03:34.826 --> 00:03:38.049
Well, the Maasai people have been living in the Serengeti for years.

44
00:03:39.170 --> 00:03:43.654
Now they are mostly cattle herdsmen, who sometimes roam with their herds to find food and water.

45
00:03:44.755 --> 00:03:49.634
According to a Maasai saying, cattle and children are the most important things to Maasai people.

46
00:03:51.356 --> 00:03:57.160
Even today many Maasai still live in traditional houses made of mud, grass, sticks, and cow dung.

47
00:03:58.140 --> 00:03:59.621
And guess who builds these houses?

48
00:04:00.802 --> 00:04:06.586
Women! The men are in charge of herding and protecting while the women's duties are house-building and cooking.

49
00:04:07.567 --> 00:04:10.109
I can't wait to hear what my mom thinks about that.

50
00:04:11.130 --> 00:04:12.090
In class Mr.

51
00:04:12.150 --> 00:04:16.213
Fay showed us lots of pictures of the Maasai, and I thought they looked cool in their red

52
00:04:16.293 --> 00:04:17.593
robes and colorful beads.

53
00:04:18.653 --> 00:04:23.395
Beaded jewelry is a symbol of age and status, and red is a symbol of courage in Maasai

54
00:04:23.435 --> 00:04:28.796
culture. They sound like some of the most courageous people on earth, living out there with the Big

55
00:04:28.896 --> 00:04:31.417
Five, forever ready to protect their cattle.

56
00:04:33.138 --> 00:04:38.639
But the Maasai way of life is slowly becoming threatened as more land in the Serengeti is preserved

57
00:04:38.719 --> 00:04:40.560
and grazing areas become restricted.

58
00:04:41.664 --> 00:04:47.208
When national parks were created there to preserve game and wildlife, the Maasai were kicked off land they

59
00:04:47.248 --> 00:04:48.349
had roamed for years.

60
00:04:49.489 --> 00:04:54.473
Mr. Fay says it's tough to balance the need to protect natural resources and wildlife with the need

61
00:04:54.513 --> 00:04:55.914
to allow people to make a living.

62
00:04:56.894 --> 00:05:02.758
Poverty in Africa and a high demand for wild meat have caused poachers, or illegal hunters, to venture

63
00:05:02.818 --> 00:05:05.200
into the protected parks of the Serengeti to hunt.

64
00:05:06.836 --> 00:05:12.439
Officials say about forty thousand to two hundred thousand animals are killed every year by poachers.

65
00:05:13.620 --> 00:05:18.062
This makes conservationists, who are trying to protect endangered species there, distraught.

66
00:05:19.063 --> 00:05:20.904
Hearing this made me pretty mad too.

67
00:05:22.045 --> 00:05:27.208
One way people try to preserve this balance of economy and ecology is by offering safari tours.

68
00:05:28.288 --> 00:05:32.350
Guiding these tours provides locals with an income that doesn't come from harming the land.

69
00:05:33.411 --> 00:05:34.752
And it's a big business!

70
00:05:35.956 --> 00:05:41.618
About ninety thousand tourists a year visit the Serengeti to "go on a safari," or ride in protected

71
00:05:41.658 --> 00:05:47.499
vehicles, hoping to catch glimpses of hunting lions, migrating wildebeests, and grazing gazelles.

72
00:05:49.500 --> 00:05:53.481
Even if you're not adventurous enough to go on a safari, you can still have a good time

73
00:05:53.541 --> 00:05:57.362
viewing birds, visiting national parks, and taking amazing photos.

74
00:05:58.622 --> 00:06:01.363
It sounds like Melissa and Alex are here, so I'd better go now.

75
00:06:02.423 --> 00:06:06.538
We're planning to work on our poster outside; that way it won't get too messy in here.

76
00:06:07.579 --> 00:06:11.143
But first I've got a question for you: now that you've learned all these cool things about the

77
00:06:11.183 --> 00:06:13.225
Serengeti, would you go on our tour?
